WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.145 --> 00:00:04.015
- [Narrator] Astronomers have
found dozens of potentially

00:00:04.015 --> 00:00:07.721
habitable planets outside
of our solar system.

00:00:07.721 --> 00:00:11.930
That's dozens of chances to
discover the first alien life.

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:12.969
Or, you know,

00:00:12.969 --> 00:00:15.162
plenty of places we could park our first

00:00:15.162 --> 00:00:17.313
interstellar colonies.

00:00:17.313 --> 00:00:20.922
But with so many options,
how do we know which is best?

00:00:20.922 --> 00:00:23.312
You might think that
most Earth-like planets

00:00:23.312 --> 00:00:25.409
should be at the top of our list.

00:00:25.409 --> 00:00:27.728
After all, we've got everything we need.

00:00:27.728 --> 00:00:30.249
Water, land, an atmosphere,

00:00:30.249 --> 00:00:33.714
and trillions of life
forms lapping it all up.

00:00:33.714 --> 00:00:36.456
But according to a small
group of researchers,

00:00:36.456 --> 00:00:39.655
there are bigger and
better planets out there.

00:00:39.655 --> 00:00:41.988
They're called super-Earths.

00:00:43.257 --> 00:00:46.074
Super-Earths may be
some of the most common

00:00:46.074 --> 00:00:48.425
planets in our galaxy.

00:00:48.425 --> 00:00:49.690
Since 2009,

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:54.690
Kepler Space Telescope has
discovered about 4000 exoplanets.

00:00:54.929 --> 00:00:57.762
30% of them are super-Earths,

00:00:58.632 --> 00:01:01.242
and a few percent of those super-Earths

00:01:01.242 --> 00:01:04.809
orbit within their host
star's habitable zone.

00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:06.433
That's the Goldilocks Zone,

00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:09.554
where the planet's surface is
just the right temperature for

00:01:09.554 --> 00:01:11.026
liquid water.

00:01:11.026 --> 00:01:14.016
Not too cold, not too hot.

00:01:14.016 --> 00:01:16.698
Now, there's a chance
some of these super-Earths

00:01:16.698 --> 00:01:19.002
aren't rocky worlds like Earth.

00:01:19.002 --> 00:01:21.409
The larger ones could
be made out of mostly

00:01:21.409 --> 00:01:23.538
hydrogen and helium gas,

00:01:23.538 --> 00:01:25.121
like Jupiter and Saturn,

00:01:25.121 --> 00:01:29.018
which would not be very
hospitable for life.

00:01:29.018 --> 00:01:30.000
But the reality is,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.201
astronomers are still gathering details

00:01:32.201 --> 00:01:34.527
as more data comes in.

00:01:34.527 --> 00:01:35.986
So, in the meantime,

00:01:35.986 --> 00:01:39.529
let's explore what life on a
rocky, habitable super-Earth

00:01:39.529 --> 00:01:40.977
might be like.

00:01:40.977 --> 00:01:43.666
Liquid water is just the start.

00:01:43.666 --> 00:01:46.874
These planets can be almost
double Earth's radius,

00:01:46.874 --> 00:01:50.305
and up to 10 times more massive.

00:01:50.305 --> 00:01:53.505
And all that extra mass
is what researchers think

00:01:53.505 --> 00:01:57.714
could really make super-
Earths the perfect home.

00:01:57.714 --> 00:01:59.808
That's because more massive planets have

00:01:59.808 --> 00:02:02.536
a stronger gravitational pull.

00:02:02.536 --> 00:02:05.682
super-Earth Kepler 20b, for example

00:02:05.682 --> 00:02:08.090
is nearly double the size of Earth,

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:11.026
and it's 10 times more massive.

00:02:11.026 --> 00:02:15.674
This makes its surface gravity
almost 3 times stronger.

00:02:15.674 --> 00:02:18.473
That stronger gravity means
that the planet can hold

00:02:18.473 --> 00:02:22.834
on to more air molecules and
form a thicker atmosphere,

00:02:22.834 --> 00:02:24.521
which is great for protecting against

00:02:24.521 --> 00:02:26.953
harmful space radiation.

00:02:26.953 --> 00:02:30.665
It also means mountains and
hills would erode a lot faster,

00:02:30.665 --> 00:02:34.631
leaving a relatively flatter
surface, compared to Earth.

00:02:34.631 --> 00:02:36.082
Now that might sound boring,

00:02:36.082 --> 00:02:38.898
but scientists think this
could actually spawn dozens

00:02:38.898 --> 00:02:42.209
of shallow islands all across the planet.

00:02:42.209 --> 00:02:43.553
Those in turn,

00:02:43.553 --> 00:02:48.545
could be the perfect place
for life to form and evolve.

00:02:48.545 --> 00:02:51.969
Just as biodiversity in
Earth's oceans is richest

00:02:51.969 --> 00:02:54.785
in shallow waters near coastlines,

00:02:54.785 --> 00:02:56.585
such an "archipelago world"

00:02:56.585 --> 00:03:00.777
might be enormously advantageous to life.

00:03:00.777 --> 00:03:02.945
There's just one problem,

00:03:02.945 --> 00:03:07.826
leaving this tropical paradise
would be extremely difficult.

00:03:07.826 --> 00:03:10.273
The escape velocity on Kepler 20b

00:03:10.273 --> 00:03:13.810
is more than double compared to Earth's,

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:16.585
which means either rockets
would need more fuel

00:03:16.585 --> 00:03:18.393
to reach their destinations...

00:03:18.393 --> 00:03:19.578
Like for example,

00:03:19.578 --> 00:03:22.002
a mission similar to
the Apollo Moon Landing

00:03:22.002 --> 00:03:24.568
would require twice the amount of fuel.

00:03:24.568 --> 00:03:27.634
Or, rockets would have
to carry only a fraction

00:03:27.634 --> 00:03:29.202
of the payload.

00:03:29.202 --> 00:03:34.096
For instance, SpaceX's Falcon
Heavy can launch 50 thousand

00:03:34.096 --> 00:03:37.850
kilograms of payload into Earth's orbit.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:40.415
Whereas it could only launch 40 kilograms

00:03:40.415 --> 00:03:43.934
into orbit around a super-
Earth like Kepler 20b.

00:03:43.934 --> 00:03:46.649
That's about the weight
of a German Shepherd.

00:03:46.649 --> 00:03:47.801
Suffice it to say,

00:03:47.801 --> 00:03:51.135
leaving a super-Earth would
be a far greater challenge.

00:03:51.135 --> 00:03:53.234
But if it looked like this,

00:03:53.234 --> 00:03:55.600
would you really want to say goodbye?

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:59.184
We'll never know for
sure until we visit one.

00:03:59.184 --> 00:04:02.009
What questions do you have about space?

00:04:02.009 --> 00:04:04.488
Leave your queries and the comments below.

00:04:04.488 --> 00:04:06.571
And thanks for watching.

